nodecode-xmemo is a rewrite of yonro's yonro/hermes-xmemo-plugin:

  https://github.com/yonro/hermes-xmemo-plugin
  commit 499a383778647e897d30db3e92e8c8ad4397c799
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 XMemo contributors (LICENSE)

The rewritten plugin is src/hermes_xmemo/xmemo of that commit. Its six core
tools (xmemo_search, xmemo_get, xmemo_list, xmemo_recall_context,
xmemo_remember, xmemo_update_state) keep their names, descriptions and
parameters; its wire keeps its shapes (X-API-Key plus the agent-id headers
against https://xmemo.dev; GET /v1/memories/search, GET
/v1/memories/<id>/explain, POST /v1/remember, POST /v1/update_state, POST
/v1/recall/context, POST /v1/timeline/events); and its behaviour is carried
over: one search across every bucket and scope the account holds, the
bilingual alias table that widens a query, the RFC 3339 filter validation
the original does before a request leaves, the bounded context pack, the
provenance labels on results, idempotency keys on writes, and the
five-failure breaker its reads pause behind for two minutes. The words the
model is given about it - the tool descriptions and the manual page - are
the original's, condensed.

What was left out, and why: the local SQLite recall cache and the durable
outbox that retries a failed write, because a cell's writes are synchronous
and the answer a write earns is the answer the model gets (POST
/v1/remember carries an idempotency key, so a retry is safe); the workflow
and destructive tools (timeline events, reminders, forget) and the feedback
tool (mark_used), which the original keeps opt-in or internal, so only its
six defaults are registered; the restart snapshot on session end and the
mirroring of Hermes's own built-in memory writes, because those are seams of
Hermes's provider interface, not of a Nodecode cell (a finished turn still
reaches the timeline when capture_timeline is on); the agent_instance_id
that the original persists in its own JSON file, replaced by an id generated
at each start; and the fallback to the XMemo CLI's credential file, because
a Nodecode cell reads its section and the environment, not another tool's
config. LICENSE is that repository's LICENSE, unchanged.
